Files: src/mmt_core/src/mmt_inet_ntop.c
Copyright: 2000 Apple Computer, Inc.
 1994, 1995 Massachusetts Institute of Technology
License: APSL-2.0 and MIT-1994
Comment:
 The current file has no header [H], but the code is the IPv6 text formatter
 (zero-run compression with zr[]/hexa[][]) that carried an Apple Public Source
 License 2.0 header plus an MIT 1994/1995 permission notice until commit
 7a6f98db / 1b02f7e0 (2017-08-28) deleted them [G]. The body is still
 derived: compare git show 1b02f7e0^:src/mmt_core/src/mmt_inet_ntop.c with
 the current file (162 lines) [G]. APSL-2.0 is file-level copyleft (modified
 Covered Code must remain APSL-2.0, source must be available) and is
 not Apache-2.0; Debian has historically accepted APSL-2.0 as DFSG-free
 [A, verify with debian-legal/ftpmasters]. Cleanest fix: replace the file with
 a small clean-room implementation (glibc already provides inet_ntop) or
 restore the notices.

Files: src/mmt_tcpip/lib/protocols/proto_citrix.c
Copyright: 2012-2022 ntop.org
 2026 Montimage
License: LGPL-3+
Comment:
 Lines 14-15 state "Based on nDPI's citrix.c detection logic (ntop/nDPI,
 LGPL-3.0)" [H]. Upstream citrix.c header: "Copyright (C) 2012-22 -
 ntop.org ... GNU Lesser General Public License ... either version 3 ... or
 (at your option) any later version", "based on the OpenDPI and PACE
 technology by ipoque GmbH" [W]. The MMT function reimplements the same byte
 signatures (0x7f7f494341 00, "CGP/01", "Citrix.TcpProxyService"); whether
 that is a protectable derivative is a judgement for the maintainer [A]. The
 file is shown as LGPL-3+ here because the file itself declares the derivation.
 Also see proto_syslog.c:91 ("nDPI-inspired") and the ~120 files that share
 the OpenDPI/nDPI structure (header comment above).

